.TH BRICK 1 "" "brick" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
brick \- storage sync CLI client for Webbite Brick
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B brick
[\fIOPTIONS\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B brick
is the command-line client for Webbite Brick's storage sync feature. It performs
two-way synchronization between a local folder and Brick: it walks both trees,
uploads local-only files, downloads remote-only files, and resolves conflicts
with the remote copy winning. After the initial pass it watches the local folder
for filesystem changes and also polls the Storage API periodically, so both
sides stay in sync until interrupted.
.PP
.B brick
also manages the OIDC login and active account used for sync.
.PP
On first run
.B brick
creates a configuration file at
.I ~/.config/brick/config.yaml
containing a unique client identifier.
Authentication tokens are stored in the same file after a successful login.
.SH OPTIONS
.SS Account management
.TP
.B \-\-login
Open the default web browser and start login flow.
On completion the received credentials are saved to the configuration file.
Run this command before using any feature that requires an authenticated account.
.TP
.B \-\-switch\-accounts
Interactively select which account to make active when more than one account
has been authenticated.
The selected account ID is persisted to the configuration file.
.TP
.B \-\-whoami
Print the name, email address, and active account of the currently authenticated
user.
If the access token has expired it is refreshed silently using the stored refresh
token.
.TP
.B \-\-restart
Clear existing settings and configure Brick from scratch, as if freshly
installed. Stops any running instance, then for each account with a
configured sync folder interactively offers to delete its local files and
the folder itself, and finally removes the entire configuration directory
before falling through into first-run setup.
.SS Storage sync
.TP
Running
.B brick
with no other options synchronizes the folder configured as
.I storageSyncFolder
in the configuration file with Brick, then watches it for changes.
If no sync folder is configured yet,
.B brick
prompts for one and persists it to the configuration file. The remote copy
always wins on conflict. Requires a logged-in session with an active account
(see
.B \-\-login
and
.BR \-\-switch\-accounts ).
Press
.B Ctrl\-C
to stop syncing. When run in an interactive terminal, the sync loop also
accepts
.BR D
to detach into the background (equivalent to
.BR \-\-daemon ,
where supported) and
.BR P
to pause or resume syncing without stopping it.
.TP
.BR \-d ", " \-\-daemon
Detach into the background once logged in and Brick's API is reachable,
so syncing continues after the terminal is closed. The same effect can also
be triggered interactively from the foreground sync loop by pressing
.BR D .
Not supported on Windows.
.TP
.BR \-r ", " \-\-remote\-control
Allow Brick to remotely list, browse, and transfer files on this device while
syncing. Without this flag the local agent refuses any such request from Brick,
even if it is otherwise connected. Can also be enabled persistently via the
configuration file, in which case this flag is unnecessary.
.TP
.B \-\-agent\-root \fIPATH\fR
Expose an additional directory (beyond
.IR storageSyncFolder )
to remote clients when
.B \-\-remote\-control
is enabled. Repeatable.
.TP
.BR \-s ", " \-\-selective\-sync
Interactively choose which top-level folders of the active account are
excluded from sync. Folders newly excluded are deleted from local disk
immediately; any running
.B brick
instance is restarted to pick up the change.
.TP
.B \-\-list\-selective\-sync
Print the folders currently excluded from sync for the active account.
.SS Other
.TP
.B \-\-no\-upgrade\-check
Suppress the automatic check for a newer version of
.B brick
that normally runs at startup before syncing.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-control\-api
Disable the local status/control API that
.B brick
otherwise starts alongside the sync loop. Local clients such as a tray app
use this API to query sync status and to send commands (pause, quit,
detach) to a running instance; with this flag such clients cannot find or
control the process.
.TP
.B \-\-uninstall
Interactively remove the
.B brick
binary, configuration directory, shell completion files, and man page.
Each step prompts for confirmation before proceeding.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print a short usage summary and exit.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-version
Print the version number, build timestamp, and git commit hash, then exit.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
Log in to a Webbite account:
.EX
brick \-\-login
.EE
.TP
Show the currently authenticated user:
.EX
brick \-\-whoami
.EE
.TP
Switch to a different account:
.EX
brick \-\-switch\-accounts
.EE
.TP
Sync the configured folder with Brick and watch for changes:
.EX
brick
.EE
.TP
Sync and allow Brick to remotely browse and transfer files:
.EX
brick \-\-remote\-control
.EE
.TP
Sync without checking for CLI upgrades first:
.EX
brick \-\-no\-upgrade\-check
.EE
.TP
Sync and immediately detach into the background:
.EX
brick \-\-daemon
.EE
.TP
Choose which folders to exclude from sync:
.EX
brick \-\-selective\-sync
.EE
.TP
List folders currently excluded from sync:
.EX
brick \-\-list\-selective\-sync
.EE
.TP
Clear all local settings and set up Brick again from scratch:
.EX
brick \-\-restart
.EE
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/brick/config.yaml
Per-user configuration file.
Created automatically on first run.
Contains the client identifier, the configured
.IR storageSyncFolder ,
and, after login, OAuth credentials.
Stored with mode
.B 0600
to protect the tokens.
Do not share this file.
.TP
.I ~/.config/brick/sync\-state\-<account\-id>.json
Per-account sync state index, one file per account ID ever synced (so two
accounts sharing the same local folder still keep independent bookkeeping).
Records the last-known local hash and remote ETag for each synced file, plus
a server-time cursor for incremental polling, so repeated runs only transfer
what changed. Safe to delete;
.B brick
will simply re-reconcile the full tree on the next run.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
An error occurred (invalid flags, network failure, authentication error, etc.).
